\subsection{The attack is invisible to accuracy monitoring}

Under undefended FedAvg the backdoor succeeds everywhere. At the headline
operating point, averaged over five seeds, FedAvg's BSR is
$\msBSRfedavgFixed\pm\msBSRsdfedavgFixed$ on the fixed trigger and
$\msBSRfedavgStatistical$ on both low-salience triggers (Table~\ref{tab:main}),
while its clean accuracy ($\msCleanCAfedavg$) is indistinguishable from the
no-attack model.
Across the broader single-seed grid every one of the $\gridAttackCells$ attack
cells has BSR $\geq\gridMinBSRfedavg$. The poisoning leaves no accuracy footprint, which is what makes it
dangerous: an operator watching validation accuracy sees a healthy detector. This
motivates aggregation-level defenses rather than accuracy monitoring.

\subsection{Headline comparison, with false positives}
\label{sec:fpr}

Table~\ref{tab:main} is the paper's central table, and it now carries FPR beside
DR. Reading it changes what two of the baselines look like.

