\textbf{FLAME's robustness is a false-positive story.} It reaches BSR $\msBSRflameFixed$ on
the fixed trigger, the second-best number in the column, while flagging
$\msFPRflameFixed\pm\msFPRsdflameFixed$ of \emph{honest} clients---and
$\msFPRflameStatistical$--$\msFPRflameTemporal$ on the other two triggers, where
its DR is only $\msDRflameStatistical$--$\msDRflameTemporal$. A defense
discarding two honest updates in five is training on roughly $60\%$ of the
federation, which is a sufficient explanation for its $\msCleanCAflame$ clean
accuracy against FedAvg's $\msCleanCAfedavg$.
This is the mechanism behind the ``no free lunch'' observation, and it is
invisible without FPR: on BSR alone FLAME looks like a near-solution.

\textbf{GradCos's detector is not merely blind on low-salience triggers---it is
uninformative.} On statistical and temporal its DR ($\msDRgradcosStatistical$,
$\msDRgradcosTemporal$) is \emph{below} its FPR ($\msFPRgradcosStatistical$,
$\msFPRgradcosTemporal$). The clique test is not failing to find the
colluders so much as flagging clients at close to chance. We think this is the
sharpest available statement of the blind spot, and it is stronger evidence than
a low DR alone would be, because it rules out the reading that the detector is
simply conservative. Fig.~\ref{fig:drfpr} plots the two rates against each other
and makes the separation visible: only GradCos on the fixed trigger reaches the
top-left corner, while its low-salience points sit on the chance diagonal and
FLAME sits far to the right on every trigger.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.68\linewidth]{fig_dr_fpr.pdf}
\caption{\textbf{Detection rate against false-positive rate} (CICIoT2023, IID,
$m{=}0.2$, $p{=}0.1$, 5 seeds). Only GradCos on the fixed trigger reaches the
top-left corner a detector should occupy. FLAME sits far right on every
trigger---its low backdoor rate is paid for by discarding honest updates---and
GradCos on the low-salience triggers sits \emph{on} the chance diagonal, flagging
honest and malicious clients at indistinguishable rates. FedAvg and the
coordinate-wise rules never flag a client and do not appear.}
\label{fig:drfpr}
\end{figure}

\textbf{FLTrust never excludes anyone.} Its DR and FPR are identically zero on
every trigger: no client's update ever receives a trust score of exactly zero.
Whatever robustness it has comes from continuous reweighting toward the server's
root direction, not from exclusion---which is also why it degrades the clean
model, since honest updates that disagree with a 300-sample root direction are
attenuated too.

\subsection{Salience decides the outcome, and it is measurable}
\label{sec:salience}

Grouped by trigger rather than by attacker count, the pattern in
Table~\ref{tab:main} is stark. On the \emph{fixed} trigger the direction- and
reference-based defenses work: GradCos $\msBSRgradcosFixed$ and FLTrust
$\msBSRfltrustFixed$ are lowest, FLAME $\msBSRflameFixed$, against FedAvg's
$\msBSRfedavgFixed$. On \emph{statistical} and \emph{temporal} they fail almost
completely---GradCos, Krum and FoolsGold all at $\msBSRgradcosStatistical$, FLAME
at $\msBSRflameStatistical$---with FLTrust on statistical
($\msBSRfltrustStatistical$) the lone exception, and it uses its clean root direction rather than inter-client
similarity.

The mechanism is visible in the update geometry we log. The fixed trigger stamps
header/flag features that few benign flows exercise, so learning it bends a
poisoned client's update in a direction honest clients never take. The
statistical and temporal triggers perturb volume, dispersion and timing features
and leave the poisoned update nearly collinear with an honest one, so the cosine
signal every one of these defenses depends on is simply not there. That FoolsGold---built
precisely to catch colluding sybils by cosine similarity---also fails
underlines that the blind spot belongs to the \emph{signal}, not to one defense's
tuning.

\textbf{Identifying the axis.} A hostile reading of the paragraph above is that
it is nearly definitional: ``direction-based defenses fail when the trigger does
not move the direction'' restates the mechanism rather than testing it. Two
things separate the claim from a tautology. First, $S$
(Eq.~\ref{eq:salience}) is measured independently of any defense's decision, so
BSR and DR can be regressed on it. Second, the three trigger families differ in
\emph{which} features they touch as well as how hard, so a two-group contrast
cannot separate feature coverage from magnitude. We therefore sweep the trigger
magnitude at a fixed feature set, $0.25\times$ to $3\times$ the base pattern, and
report BSR and DR against measured $S$ rather than against the trigger's name
% RETIRES WITH: lane 2, magnitude-sweep
(Fig.~\ref{fig:dose}). \PENDING

