We expect, and will report either way, that the residual variant is \emph{also}
vulnerable: mean-centering removes the shared task direction but the decorrelation
noise survives it, so the residual clique signal should degrade for the same
reason the full-vector one does. If so, the honest conclusion is that our
diagnosis of the blind spot is correct and our proposed fix for it is not
adaptive-secure---which is a more useful thing to report than a defense evaluated
only against the attacker it was designed for.

\subsection{Do magnitude-based rules share the blind spot?}
\label{sec:magnitude}

If low-salience triggers evade defenses by not moving the update direction, then
rules that never read direction should not fail for that reason. This is the
control our earlier framing lacked: a claim that ``the whole cosine family has a
structural blind spot'' is only interesting against comparators outside the
family. Table~\ref{tab:magnitude} reports the coordinate-wise median, trimmed
mean, RFA, Bulyan and norm-clip at the headline point on all three triggers.
