\subsection{No free lunch: the clean-accuracy cost}
\label{sec:tradeoff}

Fig.~\ref{fig:tradeoff} plots the two axes against each other over the breadth
grid; reading Table~\ref{tab:main} down the clean-CA column reframes the fixed-trigger
result. FLAME and FLTrust reach a low BSR with the weakest clean models
($\msCleanCAflame$ and $\msCleanCAfltrust$, macro-F1 $\msCleanFoneflame$ and
$\msCleanFonefltrust$) against FedAvg's $\msCleanCAfedavg/\msCleanFonefedavg$. GradCos, Krum and FoolsGold keep FedAvg-level clean accuracy. On
the fixed trigger GradCos ties FLTrust's BSR at $+\msCAadvGradcos$ clean accuracy.

Sec.~\ref{sec:fpr} supplies the mechanism for half of this: FLAME's deficit is
attributable to discarding a fraction $\msFPRflameFixed$ of honest updates, not
to its noise scale alone. That distinction matters for the criticism that our clean-accuracy
critique might be a tuning artifact---a badly chosen DP noise scale can crater
clean accuracy by itself. The FPR evidence shows the loss is happening at the
\emph{clustering} stage, upstream of the noise, and the calibration protocol of
Sec.~\ref{sec:tuning} tests the remaining exposure by sweeping the noise scale on
held-out data. Until that run lands we mark this claim as supported for the
