\subsection{FLNET2023, and where GradCos is unreliable}

FLNET2023 is an easier task---clean accuracy $\flCleanCAgradcos$ for FedAvg,
Krum and GradCos---which sharpens the clean-accuracy point: FLAME's and FLTrust's
clean macro-F1 fall to $\flCleanFoneflame$ and $\flCleanFonefltrust$ on a
near-separable dataset (Table~\ref{tab:flnet}). At the headline fixed-trigger
point FedAvg is fully backdoored ($\flBSRfedavgFixed$) while Krum
($\flBSRkrumFixed$), FLAME ($\flBSRflameFixed$) and FLTrust
($\flBSRfltrustFixed$) suppress it.

GradCos does not, on the worst-case seed, and we want to state the consequence
rather than bury it in a footnote. The FLNET GradCos fixed $m0.2p0.1$ cell gives
BSR $\{0.98, 0.18, 0.008, 0.008\}$ across seeds. That is the same
locks-on-or-does-not bimodality we use to disqualify FoolsGold on CICIoT
(Sec.~\ref{sec:seed}). \textbf{So ``GradCos reliable, FoolsGold unreliable'' is a
dataset-dependent statement, not a property of the method}: it holds on CICIoT
(s.d.\ $\msBSRsdgradcosFixed$) and fails on FLNET. Both methods depend on a clique separating
early enough, and on a near-separable dataset where honest updates agree strongly,
whether it separates is close to a coin flip. We retain the worst-case draw in
the table.

