\subsection{Non-IID}

At the single non-IID (Dirichlet, $\alpha=0.5$) configuration we test---fixed
trigger, $m=0.2,p=0.1$ (Table~\ref{tab:noniid})---GradCos is the strongest
defense: BSR
$\niBSRgradcosFixed$ at clean accuracy $\niCleanCAgradcos$. FedAvg is backdoored
($\niBSRfedavgFixed$) and so is Krum ($\niBSRkrumFixed$):
heterogeneous honest data already spreads updates out, which blunts Krum's
distance test.

We previously wrote that this shows ``the mechanism survives heterogeneity.'' It
does not show that. One Dirichlet concentration, one seed, one trigger and one
operating point cannot characterize behavior in the setting that matters most
for a federated IDS, and the one thing this slice does establish---that
heterogeneity flips Krum from robust to backdoored---is itself evidence that
non-IID data changes the geometry these defenses read. The central claim of this
paper is therefore \emph{untested} under realistic heterogeneity, and we say so.
A sweep over $\alpha\in\{0.1,0.5,1.0\}$ across all three triggers is in the
